<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Colonial Nigeria</h1><p class="st-meta">1914 CE – 1960 CE · capital Lagos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The British colony formed in 1914 by joining the northern and southern protectorates, the most populous colony in Africa and independent as Nigeria in 1960.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>On 1 January 1914 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-lugard">Frederick Lugard</a> amalgamated the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/protectorate-of-northern-nigeria">Protectorate of Northern Nigeria</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/protectorate-of-southern-nigeria">Protectorate of Southern Nigeria</a> into the Colony and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-lagos">Lagos</a>. The move mainly allowed the revenues of the south to cover the costs of the north; the two halves continued to be administered largely separately.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>British rule relied on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indirect-rule">indirect rule</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> and chiefs, extended to the south through appointed warrant chiefs, a policy that provoked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aba-womens-war">Aba Women's War</a> of 1929. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> exported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a>, cocoa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundnut">groundnuts</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a>. Nationalist politics grew around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herbert-macaulay">Herbert Macaulay</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nnamdi-azikiwe">Nnamdi Azikiwe</a>, and the constitutions of 1946, 1951 and 1954 created a federation of Northern, Western and Eastern regions.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The regions became self-governing in the late 1950s, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmadu-bello">Ahmadu Bello</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obafemi-awolowo">Obafemi Awolowo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nnamdi-azikiwe">Azikiwe</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abubakar-tafawa-balewa">Abubakar Tafawa Balewa</a> became federal prime minister in 1957, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> became independent on 1 October 1960.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/colonial-nigeria.jpg" alt="Colonial Nigeria" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Fry1989 eh? · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Nigeria_(1952%E2%80%931960).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1914 CE – 1960 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lagos</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Frederick Lugard, Hugh Clifford, Abubakar Tafawa Balewa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam, Christianity, traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Hausa, Yoruba, Igbo</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Colony and Protectorate of Nigeria, British Nigeria, Nigeria (colonial)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1937&amp;focus=colonial-nigeria" aria-label="Colonial Nigeria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4774 1936 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Colonial Nigeria lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5094 2192L5109 2141M5094 2192L5093 2166M5094 2192L5094 2192M5094 2192L5170 2068M5094 2192L4997 651M5094 2192L5256 2278M5094 2192L5362 1992M5094 2192L5156 2197"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5109 2141h0M5093 2166h0M5094 2192h0M5170 2068h0M4997 651h0M5256 2278h0M5362 1992h0M5156 2197h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5094 2192h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5094 2192h0"/></svg>